From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] advapi32: privilege display names, Safer levels, security down a tree

- LookupPrivilegeDisplayNameA/W failed: they give the user right each
  privilege grants, as Windows' documentation names them, with the length
  needed for a small buffer and ERROR_NO_SUCH_PRIVILEGE for an unknown one.
- GetExplicitEntriesFromAclA failed: the entries name trustees by SID,
  which is the same in either character set.
- The Safer level functions handed out 0xdeadbeef as level and token, so
  "run as a normal user" launchers (runas /trustlevel, installers dropping
  elevation) failed to start anything. A level is a handle of its own; a
  normal user's token is the caller's with Administrators for deny only,
  only the bypass-traverse privilege and medium integrity; a constrained
  one is also restricted to the user, Everyone, Users and RESTRICTED; an
  untrusted one runs at low integrity; disallowed software gets none.
  SaferGetLevelInformation is exported; with no restriction policies,
  SaferIdentifyLevel and the default level say fully trusted.
- TreeSetNamedSecurityInfoW failed and TreeResetNamedSecurityInfoW did
  nothing. The root gets what is given; every file or key below gets the
  owner and group given and its explicit entries (none when resetting)
  plus what it inherits from its parent, a protected ACL kept as it is;
  the progress callback is called per object and may cancel.

Wine's advapi32 security tests: the same failures as before, no new ones.

Gate: test/advsec-gate.sh. Mutants SG_MUTANT_NO_PRIVILEGE_NAMES,
SG_MUTANT_NO_SAFER, SG_MUTANT_NO_TREE_SECURITY (advapi32/security.c).
